"Easy, Durn." The words seem to steady the man on the other side of the bend. There is a shaky breath, then he answers.

"Can't walk," Durn says at last. "Left leg's broke. That light showed us a seam — silver, bright as running water — down a drift that weren't on any map. The floor gave way under Arris. I went for him, and it gave way under me too." He stops to breathe, ragged. "There's a hole past this bend, a straight drop — the old winze. Arris and Keld are at the bottom of it. I caught a timber and dragged myself back here." Another breath. "So between you and me, friend: that light, and the edge of the hole. Don't trust the floor, and don't watch the light. It shows you things you want."

At the bend, the pale glow still hangs, motionless now, brightening and dimming like slow breathing, waiting to see which way this goes.


>>>>>> round 8, the player: "Understood, Durn. Are Arris and Keld alive down there, did you hear them after the fall?" While I wait, I crouch and test the floor ahead with the butt of the miner's pick, keeping my eyes on the stone and off the light, and I look for a safe path along the wall toward him.

  [dice Brakka's Wisdom (Survival) check to find a safe path along the wall toward Durn, testing the floor: 1d20+2 = 1d20 [8] + 2 = 10]
  [time: 1 minute later: Day 1, 5:44 PM]

"I called down till I had no voice left," Durn says quietly. "There was a moan, once, right after — then nothing. That's three days gone. No man lives at the bottom of a winze that long. Arris and Keld are gone."

The pick rings dull against the center of the floor — hollow, ready to go. Near the bend the floor is already gone, a ragged black mouth in the stone where the old winze drops away out of sight. Along the left wall the stone sounds truer under the pick, and the timber props stand closer together there, but the wood is old and powder-white, and Brakka cannot be certain it will bear his weight in mail. It is the only way forward, though: the hole spans the middle of the gallery, and Durn lies against the far wall, just past the bend, on the other side of it.

Over the mouth of the winze the pale light hangs, patient as ever, brightening and dimming like slow breath.


>>>>>> round 9, the player: "I'm sorry about Arris and Keld, Durn. Hold on, I'm coming to you." I take the lantern off my belt and loop the rope or strap if I have one around the sturdiest timber prop for a tether; if not, I test each prop with the pick before I trust it, then ease along the left wall one step at a time with my shield on my back and my free hand on the stone, eyes never on the light.

  [dice Brakka's Dexterity (Acrobatics) check, edging along the weakened floor to Durn: 1d20 = 1d20 [7] = 7]

The tether is set — the rope looped around the stoutest of the old timbers and drawn snug. He shrugs the shield onto his back, sets his free hand to the cold stone, and steps out along the wall, keeping his eyes on the rock and the floor and anything at all but the light.

He has gone perhaps ten careful paces when the floor lies to him. A timber groans, the stone under his foot crumbles like old biscuit, and the world drops out from beneath him. The rope snaps taut with a violence that slams him into the wall and drives the breath from his chest. He hangs, spinning slowly, over the black mouth of the winze, the rope biting the prop above him.

"Brakka!" Durn's voice cracks out of the dark. "Brakka, you still there?"

And the light comes drifting toward him. It swells as it nears, until the whole shaft is full of that cold greenish glow, and then it stops just out of arm's reach, hanging there, watching him dangle — bright and patient and terribly interested.


>>>>>> round 10, the player: "Still here, Durn! Don't talk, just hold tight." I squeeze my eyes shut against the glow, grab the rope with both hands, and start hauling myself up hand over hand, pressing my boots against the wall to climb back toward the timber prop.

  [dice Brakka's Strength (Athletics) check to haul himself up the rope: 1d20+4 = 1d20 [18] + 4 = 22]
