Braced against the solid stone, he takes two quick steps and leaps. Five feet is nothing to a dwarf with a run at it; he clears the gap and lands square on the far ledge, near enough now to make out Durn's huddled shape against the wall.

But the light does not wait. It blazes up — a cold, furious white-green that fills the whole gallery — and comes at him with a sound like a gale through old timbers. A bolt of lightning cracks out of it, bright and deafening, and passes so close the hair stands on his arms; it smashes into the stone at his side, and the smell of scorched rock rolls over him.

The thing hangs in the air before him now, not three feet off, its light roiling. Behind him, Durn lets out a strangled cry.
