Brakka sets himself between the light and the huddled miner and swings, squinting against the glare. The blade cuts a clean arc — and passes through the thing as if through standing water, meeting a queer cold resistance and no flesh at all. The light ripples around the steel, unbothered, and its glow flares to answer him.

Another bolt of lightning cracks out of it, close enough to make the dark leap, and smashes into the stone beyond him. The gallery stinks of scorched rock. The thing hangs before him, patient and furious at once, its light seething.

Durn's breath rasps behind him. "It won't bleed, Brakka! It ain't a body — it's a light what feeds!"

The thing drifts a half-step back, its glow brightening and dimming, watching him over the black mouth of the winze.


