Brakka's stroke goes wide — the light flits aside faster than the steel can follow — and in the same breath it lunges.

The bolt takes him full in the chest. It is not a burn so much as a blow; it slams through the mail like a hammer of white fire, and the world whites out for a heartbeat. His teeth snap together, the taste of copper floods his mouth, and he staggers back a step, boots scraping on the very lip of the winze. Smoke curls from the scorched rings of his mail. His heart hammers against his ribs as if trying to break free.

The light hangs over him, blazing now, feeding on his hurt. Behind him, Durn shouts something he cannot make out past the ringing in his ears.

Brakka's grip on the sword is white-knuckled. He can feel the cold of the shaft at his heels, and the thing is rising to strike again.


