The swing goes wide — the light flits sideways at the last instant, and Brakka's blade rings off the stone of the wall, jarring his arm to the shoulder. In the same breath the thing spits a bolt, but it too goes astray, crackling past him and dying against the far timbers in a shower of sparks.

The light sways in the air before him, guttering low and then flaring, low and then flaring, like a failing heart. It is wounded now, its radiance thin, but it has not gone out, and its patience — that terrible patience — has curdled into something like hate.

It hangs there over the black mouth of the winze, and waits for him to move.


>>>>>> round 20, the player: "Not done yet, Durn." I keep my squint, hold the sound ledge, and swing at the heart of the guttering glow again. "Come on, you cold thing, burn out!"

