The blade passes so close it shears a thread of light from the thing's edge — the glow ripples and flinches, and for a heartbeat Brakka is sure he has it — but the stroke comes up just short, and the light reforms a handspan beyond the steel.

Its answer is wild, a bolt that cracks into the floor at his feet and sends chips of stone skipping past his ankles. The thing is frantic now, its light boiling and thinning by turns, and still it will not break.

Brakka's breath rasps in his throat. His arms are heavy with the mail, and the thing hangs there, fast as a thought, wearing him down one heartbeat at a time.
